WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.800
While U.S. Agent is a significant&nbsp;
player in the comic book universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.040
he's not a household name among mainstream&nbsp;
Marvel fans. And that's why we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.920
run down some of his weirdest and wildest stories.&nbsp;
Here's the untold truth of Marvel's U.S. Agent.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.440
In Captain America #323, readers&nbsp;
are introduced to Super-Patriot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:27.040
the original superheroic identity of U.S.&nbsp;
Agent. As Super-Patriot, John Walker seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.480
like the exact opposite of the Captain America&nbsp;
that readers know. While Cap was often thoughtful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.760
and aware of the responsibility of his uniform,&nbsp;
Super-Patriot ignores an old lady being mugged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.800
because the crime isn't glitzy enough for him&nbsp;
to bother to stop it. He's first introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.600
trash-talking Cap and urging the American&nbsp;
people to focus their patriotic energy on him.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.160
As you might expect, Cap's not thrilled about&nbsp;
the upstart new hero, but he's willing to let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.520
Super-Patriot express his first amendment rights&nbsp;
... until he finds out that Super-Patriot and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:01.200
cronies are manufacturing events&nbsp;
to make themselves look better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.440
After Super-Patriot and his Buckies,&nbsp;
short for "Bold Urban Commandos,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:10.320
cause enough trouble for Cap, Steve&nbsp;
Rogers and John Walker finally face off.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.720
Somewhat surprisingly, Walker's not all&nbsp;
bluster. He's got super strength and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.440
enough fight training that he actually battles&nbsp;
Cap to a draw. The fight only ends when Bruce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.080
Springsteen shows up, causing Walker to choose&nbsp;
listening to the Boss rather than beating up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.960
on Captain America. As the old saying goes, if&nbsp;
you want to be the man, you gotta beat the man.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.170
"You just don't know when to give up, do ya?"

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.040
"I could do this all day."

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.080
Captain America has to deal with a lot of stuff,&nbsp;
like Hydra or the Winter Soldier, but as it turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:46.080
out, he's got even bigger problems. The man owes&nbsp;
back taxes to the U.S. government. Combined with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:51.200
the fact that his superhero identity, powers,&nbsp;
and unbreakable shield are all the property of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.000
the federal government, a federal council called&nbsp;
the Commission tells the hero that he's no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.880
able to keep operating as an independent&nbsp;
entity. The Commission offers him a choice:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.240
Work under their purview, or give up being&nbsp;
Captain America. Steve walks out on them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:11.840
choosing to adopt a black costume and the moniker&nbsp;
of the Captain in order to keep his independence.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:17.520
In Captain America #333, the Commission taps&nbsp;
Super-Patriot to replace Cap, training him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.800
fight smarter and work harder. One of Walker's&nbsp;
Buckies, Lemar Hoskins, gets the opportunity to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.360
join the new Cap as his partner, while the other&nbsp;
two are left on their lonesome to become vaguely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.480
self-interested supervillains called Left-Winger&nbsp;
and Right-Winger. Walker's willing to prove that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.920
he's ready for the big time, but he's not quite&nbsp;
the pure-hearted patriot that his predecessor was.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.240
Unlike the original Cap, Walker's got&nbsp;
some serious anger issues stemming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.880
a brother who died in Vietnam and his time&nbsp;
getting experimented on by the Power Broker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:51.040
the man who gave Walker, Hoskins, and&nbsp;
the other Buckies their super strength.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.960
Heavy is the head that wears Captain America's&nbsp;
little head wings. Almost immediately after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.440
taking on the role of Captain America,&nbsp;
Walker runs into trouble. His former pals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:07.440
Left-Winger and Right-Winger, reveal his secret&nbsp;
identity to the world in Captain America #341.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.560
Four issues later, his parents are killed by&nbsp;
the Watchdogs, a militant group of domestic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:18.320
super-terrorists obsessed with keeping America&nbsp;
free of "filth." Walker's response is to kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.960
a bunch of the Watchdogs, and then hunt down&nbsp;
Left-Winger and Right-Winger to cripple them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:29.040
a massive oil tanker explosion. That's not exactly&nbsp;
acceptable behavior for a Captain America, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:34.000
Commission keeps him on anyway. And there's a good&nbsp;
reason for that. Perennial thorn in the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.520
Cap's side, the Red Skull, engineered the entire&nbsp;
string of events, manipulating the Commission,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.880
Walker, and even the Watchdogs, all in order&nbsp;
to drag Cap's good name through the mud.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.680
It all comes to a head in Captain America&nbsp;
#350, when the Captain and Walker team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.400
up to take down the Red Skull. When the dust&nbsp;
settles, Walker is impressed with the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:00.000
Cap's convictions and skill. When the Commission&nbsp;
begs Rogers to take back the suit, he refuses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.800
but Walker convinces him to return, admitting that&nbsp;
the job was harder than he thought it would be.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.960
Captain America #351 sees Walker return the&nbsp;
Captain America suit and shield to Rogers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.920
during a press event, but the celebration is cut&nbsp;
short when Walker is brutally shot by a Watchdog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:19.520
who is soon killed himself by&nbsp;
the homicidal vigilante, Scourge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.080
Lemar Hoskins, Walker's former partner now&nbsp;
operating under the moniker Battlestar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.160
finds something suspicious about the series&nbsp;
of events, eventually discovering that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.360
Watchdog who murdered Walker wasn't a real member&nbsp;
of the organization. Instead, he was a government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:38.800
agent who faked Walker's death to give the former&nbsp;
Captain America time to undergo memory implants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:44.560
plastic surgery, and vocal lessons in order&nbsp;
to begin a new identity as Jack Daniels, aka&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.640
U.S. Agent. However, it's not quite a completely&nbsp;
fresh start, as his new outfit looks just like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.360
Steve Rogers' "the Captain" suit. Plus,&nbsp;
he's still got a vibranium replica shield.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:05:00.640
The Commission puts U.S. Agent to work as their&nbsp;
man on the inside for the West Coast Avengers — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:05.920
group of also-rans operating out of Los Angeles&nbsp;
instead of New York. While on the team, U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.840
Agent doesn't exactly make friends. But he still&nbsp;
joins the team in fighting Master Pandemonium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.240
a former movie executive who made a deal with&nbsp;
the devil and stole the Scarlet Witch's imaginary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:22.000
babies to use as his arms in Hell. That's not&nbsp;
exactly the type of threat that a super strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.880
patriot is equipped to handle. Around this time,&nbsp;
U.S. Agent's old friend Battlestar shows up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:31.920
restore his memories and remind him that he's&nbsp;
not just a government stooge. Since that also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:37.600
means remembering that his parents are dead,&nbsp;
it's a bit of a mixed blessing for John/Jack.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:41.040
It might seem like U.S. Agent only&nbsp;
battles against Captain America's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:45.680
arch-enemies alongside Cap's old friends,&nbsp;
while never having any adventures of his own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.880
but that's not exactly true. In the U.S. Agent&nbsp;
miniseries, John Walker battles against Scourge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:56.800
the vigilante that shot the man who pretended to&nbsp;
shoot Walker way back in Captain America #351.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:01.040
Yeah, we know, it's kind of confusing,&nbsp;
but it gets crazier. As it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.600
this Scourge isn't the same guy as the one&nbsp;
before. Instead, Scourge is an identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.960
offered up to basically anyone who wants it. Each&nbsp;
Scourge is given the same training, information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:15.520
and resources to go around and murder&nbsp;
criminals, kind of like the Punisher.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:20.240
However, when the newest Scourge, Priscilla&nbsp;
Lyons, is tasked with gunning down a bad guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:25.360
she suddenly decides she can't do it, as it&nbsp;
seems the criminal has genuinely reformed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.600
Of course, the rest of the Scourge aren't&nbsp;
pleased about this, so Lyons begs U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.720
Agent to help her stay alive. But after they team&nbsp;
up to find the source of the Scourge organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:39.360
U.S. Agent is captured, drugged, and&nbsp;
eventually released by the Scourge trainer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:44.480
Bloodstain. Bloodstain then reveals that he's&nbsp;
actually Mike Walker, John's brother who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:49.760
supposedly killed in Vietnam, but that ends&nbsp;
up being a mind game to confuse U.S. Agent.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:53.160
"I missed out on the whole thing to be&nbsp;
honest with you. I was confused by it. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.880
was confused by the whole&nbsp;
thing, if we're being honest.”

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:00.800
Ultimately, Walker finds the&nbsp;
true mastermind behind Scourge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:05.200
a decrepit old man who used to be&nbsp;
the old Marvel hero, the Angel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.600
not to be confused with the X-Man of the&nbsp;
same name. Bloodstain dies in the fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:15.840
and U.S. Agent leaves the old man trapped and&nbsp;
helpless on the lawn of his mansion. Day saved?

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:21.280
Nothing lasts forever, and that includes the&nbsp;
West Coast Avengers. The team comes to an end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:27.040
in West Coast Avengers #102, which finds the&nbsp;
main Avengers team criticizing and ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:33.680
firing everyone in the spinoff group. U.S. Agent&nbsp;
takes the team's dissolution hard, throwing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:38.000
shield and costume into the Hudson River. Luckily&nbsp;
for him, it doesn't take long for most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:44.160
West Coast Avengers to reform into a new group&nbsp;
created by Iron Man, a team called Force Works.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:49.280
U.S. Agent keeps his codename, but he finally&nbsp;
dons a costume that wasn't formerly worn by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:54.320
Captain America. Unfortunately, it's terrible.&nbsp;
It does come with some new weapons, though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:59.680
To replace the vibranium shield he threw into the&nbsp;
Hudson, U.S. Agent is given photon gauntlets that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:06.240
can create a laser shield and shoot laser blasts.&nbsp;
Together with Scarlet Witch, Iron Man, Wonder Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:12.400
Spider-Woman, and an interdimensional alien named&nbsp;
Century, Force Works battles against a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:19.120
aliens and dream monsters, proving that no idea&nbsp;
or team name was too crazy to publish in the '90s.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:23.840
For someone who's been around the Marvel&nbsp;
universe for as long as John Walker has,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.880
he doesn't have the best track&nbsp;
record for keeping friends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.240
That's probably because anytime a former pal of&nbsp;
his does anything even remotely questionable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.320
Walker is the first one showing up to&nbsp;
berate them and send them to prison.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:41.120
That's certainly the case when Hawkeye takes over&nbsp;
the Thunderbolts, a team of former supervillains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:46.160
who want to start living on the right side of the&nbsp;
law. People aren't exactly thrilled that Hawkeye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:50.960
himself a former criminal, and a bunch of former&nbsp;
supervillains are hanging out in Colorado.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:56.240
Their presence leads Walker to become the leader&nbsp;
of the Jury, a corporate-owned superhero team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.600
that transitions over from symbiote-hunting and&nbsp;
bothering Venom to taking down the Thunderbolts.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.560
Since the Thunderbolts are the team with their&nbsp;
name on the comic cover, they handily trounce the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:12.160
Jury, eventually sending U.S. Agent bouncing back&nbsp;
around the Marvel universe to look for a new job.

00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:18.480
John Walker pops up again during Marvel's event&nbsp;
comic Maximum Security, which sees the former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.840
Super-Patriot becoming a superhuman bounty hunter,&nbsp;
tracking down supervillain bail-jumpers under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:29.200
the organization S.T.A.R.S. The job also comes&nbsp;
with a new costume, which changes the Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:35.360
America-esque outfit into a cross between a riot&nbsp;
cop and Judge Dredd, complete with an electrically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:41.200
charged riot baton and another photon shield.&nbsp;
Walker's new career is complicated somewhat by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:46.320
the revelation that Earth itself has become an&nbsp;
interplanetary prison for the worst criminals in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:52.160
the galaxy. That situation is eventually resolved,&nbsp;
but Walker sticks around, continuing to hunt down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.880
super criminals while trying to prove himself as&nbsp;
something more than just a Captain America copy.

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:01.600
Considering how that led to a three-issue&nbsp;
miniseries, followed by a brief stint where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:07.040
wears a fake Captain America costume in order to&nbsp;
be a part of the New Invaders, an updated version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:12.240
of the World War II team of Captain America,&nbsp;
Namor, Bucky, and the original Human Torch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:17.120
his efforts to get away from the Steve Rogers&nbsp;
comparisons haven't worked out that well.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:18.320
"That is America's ass."

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:24.320
During the Civil War comic event,&nbsp;
John Walker works for Tony Stark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:28.320
on the side of the government registration&nbsp;
for super-powered beings. One side effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:33.040
of registration is that villains begin fleeing&nbsp;
across the border to Canada in order to escape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:37.920
Stark's iron grip. Stark assigns Walker&nbsp;
to head up the new Omega Flight program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:42.720
which is necessary given that Alpha Flight has&nbsp;
been killed by a mutant named Michael Pointer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:47.840
who was possessed by aliens in a bid to take over&nbsp;
Earth. Walker initially refuses the assignment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:52.080
but changes his mind after getting&nbsp;
thrown off a building by the Purple Man.

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:58.320
Walker's new team consists of Julia "Arachne"&nbsp;
Carpenter, Talisman, Sasquatch, Beta Ray Bill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:04.560
and Weapon Omega... a.k.a. Michael Pointer.&nbsp;
Talisman is outraged that the man who killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:09.280
her father is on the team, but Pointer proves&nbsp;
himself in a battle against a demon-powered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:13.440
Wrecking Crew. Walker remains his usual&nbsp;
obnoxious self in leading the team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.520
but the job seems to agree with him. As&nbsp;
always, though, it doesn't last long.

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:23.440
John Walker has spent most of his career trying&nbsp;
to differentiate himself from Steve Rogers. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:27.760
here's the good news. U.S. Agent eventually&nbsp;
does find a way to distinguish himself from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:32.720
Captain America. But here's the bad news. It&nbsp;
comes with him getting his arm and leg cut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:38.240
off by the supervillain Nuke during Siege, an&nbsp;
event comic that sees Norman Osborn declaring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:42.640
war on Asgard. Walker's given the option of&nbsp;
high-tech prosthetics that would make Luke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:47.840
Skywalker envious, but he accepts his missing&nbsp;
limbs and ultimately retires from active duty.

00:11:47.840 --> 00:11:51.840
In the interim, he becomes the warden&nbsp;
of the supervillain prison, the Raft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:57.200
assisting Luke Cage in the formation of a new&nbsp;
team of Thunderbolts, which uses more traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:02.960
supervillains as a kind of probationary&nbsp;
superhero team for extremely dangerous missions.

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:05.920
"What, so we're some kind of suicide squad?"

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:07.120
Kind of!

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:11.120
That group eventually gives way to the Dark&nbsp;
Avengers, a team of villains pretending to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:16.400
be their superheroic rivals. While on a mission,&nbsp;
Walker and the Dark Avengers are teleported into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:20.800
an alternate reality where, after being&nbsp;
mistaken for that reality's John Walker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:26.000
U.S. Agent gets his limbs restored. After making&nbsp;
it back to their home reality, the team vows to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:30.480
stick together through thick and thin, which&nbsp;
doesn't really last beyond that final issue.

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:36.960
Regardless of what team he's on or what the status&nbsp;
quo of the Marvel Universe might be, U.S. Agent is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:42.160
inextricably tied to Captain America. That's to&nbsp;
be expected when even his post-Captain America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:47.520
identity still has an identical shield and&nbsp;
Cap's unused costume. But as it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:52.160
that connection is consistent even when it's&nbsp;
not Steve Rogers wearing the stars and stripes.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:57.120
When Sam Wilson, the Falcon, takes over for&nbsp;
Captain America after Steve is turned into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.920
old man by a supervillain, U.S. Agent steps&nbsp;
in to convince Sam to give up the shield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:06.320
Despite his lack of super strength, the new&nbsp;
Captain America is able to take down the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:12.080
Agent by dumping him into a pitch-black tunnel and&nbsp;
whaling on him until Walker is out for the count.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:16.960
Still, as with any hero vs. hero conflict,&nbsp;
there's a deeper conspiracy going on.

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:21.440
U.S. Agent wasn't just encouraged to&nbsp;
attack Sam Wilson because a gung-ho&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:26.000
talk show radio host told him to. As it turns&nbsp;
out, Steve Rogers actually put him up to it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:30.160
because of some complicated shenanigans&nbsp;
involving the Red Skull, the Cosmic Cube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:36.240
and a massively controversial retcon that saw&nbsp;
Steve become a secret Hydra loyalist. As with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:40.560
most comic storylines, it eventually gets&nbsp;
reverted back into the normal status quo.

